  annotation = {中文辅助译名：Vec2Null：结构化身份矢量化和一次性面部遗忘的归零},
  abstract = {Machine unlearning has emerged as a critical mechanism for personal privacy compliance. However, most existing methods require access to the original training dataset, which is impractical under stringent data regulations. To address this, we investigate the practical problem ofone-shot personal identity unlearning, where an unlearning request is fulfilled using exclusively a new face image provided by the user. In this regime, existing methods are ill-posed: optimizing directly on a single, noisy support view fails to generalize across the target identity’s manifold, inevitably resulting in either incomplete erasure or utility degradation. To bridge thissingle-view to manifold gap, we propose Vec2Null, a geometry-guided framework for one-shot identity unlearning on face-analytics models. Instead of unlearning directly from the support image, Vec2Null first vectorizes the single-shot support image into a stable identity-level direction and then performs a retain-safe nulling update. We first introduce an Unlearning-Oriented Face Representation Network (UnoFace) optimized via a novel asymmetric One-Shot Proxy objective. UnoFace maps arbitrary single views to stable, low-variance proxy directions for the target identity manifold. Building upon this, Vec2Null employs a context-aware meta-learner to synthesize robust parameter updates by fusing the target model’s internal response with the stable unlearning directions provided by UnoFace. Finally, the unlearning updates are appli},
}

